/-- The best guaranteed EF1 factor α_RR(n) for n agents under Round-Robin, defined as the supremum of guaranteed factors α. -/
noncomputable def round_robin_alpha (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] : ℝ :=
  sSup {α : ℝ | is_guaranteed_round_robin_ef1_factor n α}

/-- A picking sequence of length m for n agents is recursively balanced if every complete round of n turns is a permutation of the agents and the final incomplete round has no repeated agents. -/
def is_recursively_balanced (n : ℕ) (seq : List (Fin n)) : Prop :=
  ∀ s : ℕ, ((seq.drop (s * n)).take n).Nodup

declarations.reading:
For a given number of agents `n : ℕ`, picking sequence `seq : List (Fin n)`, and real factor `α : ℝ`, `is_guaranteed_sequence_ef1_factor n seq α` asserts:

For all valuation weight profiles `w : Fin n → Fin seq.length → ℝ`,
if all weights are non-negative (for all agents `i` and goods `g`, `0 ≤ w i g`),
then for all ranking strategy profiles `rank : Fin n → (Fin seq.length ≃ Fin seq.length)`,
if `rank` is a pure Nash equilibrium of the picking sequence game (that is, for every agent `i` and every alternate ranking permutation `r`, agent `i`'s additive valuation for their bundle under unilateral deviation to `r` is at most their valuation under `rank`),
then the resulting allocation satisfies `α`-EF1 (envy-freeness up to one good scaled by `α`): for every pair of distinct agents `i ≠ j`, whenever agent `j`'s bundle is nonempty, there exists a good `g` in `j`'s bundle such that `α` times agent `i`'s valuation of `(j's bundle \ {g})` is at most agent `i`'s valuation of `i`'s own bundle.

