Given:
- natural numbers $n, m$ with $n \ne 0$ and $m \le n$;
- a preference profile $\text{pref} : \text{Fin } n \to (\text{Fin } m \simeq \text{Fin } m)$ assigning to each agent $i$ a ranking (permutation) of items;
- a valuation function $v : \text{Fin } n \to \text{Fin } m \to \mathbb{R}$;
- an assumption that all valuations are non-negative ($v(i, g) \ge 0$ for all $i, g$);
- an assumption that valuations strictly decrease with rank in $\text{pref}$ (for every agent $i$ and indices $k < l$ in $\text{Fin } m$, $v(i, \text{pref}(i)(l)) < v(i, \text{pref}(i)(k))$);
- a sequence of bid profiles $f : \mathbb{N} \to (\text{Fin } n \to (\text{Fin } m \simeq \text{Fin } m))$ starting at truthful preferences ($f(0) = \text{pref}$);
- and a natural number $T$ such that for all $t < T$, the step from $f(t)$ to $f(t+1)$ is an iterated round-robin step (`irr_step`) with valuation $v$, picking sequence `round_robin_sequence n m`, and admissible bids given by item-preserving swaps (`is_item_preserving_swap_bid`);

the theorem asserts that $T = 0$ (i.e., no such step can take place).

